WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.962
This example shows several aggregate functions in
one query That is common

00:00:04.975 --> 00:00:08.938
in analytical SQL because one source table can
support several useful measures

00:00:08.962 --> 00:00:12.675
at the same time COUNT star gives us the number of
sales

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:18.588
rows SUM quantity adds all units sold AVG price
calculates

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.012
the average price across the rows MIN price finds
the lowest price

00:00:23.038 --> 00:00:27.388
and MAX price finds the highest price Notice that
average price is

00:00:27.412 --> 00:00:32.162
rounded to two decimal places for presentation The
ROUND function does not

00:00:32.175 --> 00:00:36.825
change the stored prices It only formats the
calculated result to a

00:00:36.838 --> 00:00:42.288
more convenient precision Run the query and compare
the measures A row

00:00:42.312 --> 00:00:45.950
count and a unit count are not the same thing One
sale

00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:50.812
row could contain a quantity greater than one
Similarly average price does

00:00:50.825 --> 00:00:56.300
not tell us total revenue Revenue usually requires
multiplying quantity by price

00:00:56.325 --> 00:01:00.138
and then summing that expression This is a good
example of why

00:01:00.162 --> 00:01:06.500
analysts should label output columns clearly Names
such as sale_rows units_sold

00:01:06.625 --> 00:01:12.288
average_price lowest_price and highest_price tell
another reader what each number

00:01:12.312 --> 00:01:17.088
means When designing a summary choose measures that
directly answer the business

00:01:17.112 --> 00:01:20.900
or research question rather than adding aggregates
simply because they are available
